<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/u-kala"><span class="enc-name">U Kala</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">U Kala was a Burmese chronicler of the late Toungoo period who compiled the Maha Yazawin, the first national chronicle of Burma, completed around 1724.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/u-nu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/u-nu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">U Nu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">U Nu (1907–1995) was a Burmese nationalist and statesman who became the first prime minister of independent Burma in 1948.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/u-thant"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/u-thant.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">U Thant</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">U Thant was a Burmese diplomat who served as Secretary-General of the United Nations from 1961 to 1971, the first Asian to hold the post.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/u-boat"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/u-boat.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">U-boat</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">U-boat is the English name for German military submarines, from the German Unterseeboot.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/u-n-dhebar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/u-n-dhebar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">U. N. Dhebar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">U.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ua-conchobair-dynasty"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ua-conchobair-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ua Conchobair dynasty</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ua Conchobair, later O'Conor, were a dynasty of the Uí Briúin who ruled the kingdom of Connacht from the 11th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ua-faelain"><span class="enc-name">Ua Fáeláin</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ua Fáeláin were the ruling family of the Déisi of Munster, in what is now County Waterford and south Tipperary, in the 11th and 12th centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ubaid-period"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ubaid-period.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ubaid period</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ubaid period is a prehistoric period of Mesopotamia, from about 6500 to 3800 BCE, named after Tell al-Ubaid near Ur.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ubar-tutu"><span class="enc-name">Ubar-Tutu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ubar-Tutu is the last king before the flood in the Sumerian King List, said to have ruled at Shuruppak for thousands of years.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ubashi-khan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ubashi-khan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ubashi Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ubashi Khan was the last khan of the Kalmyk Khanate on the lower Volga, ruling from 1761.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ubayd-allah-ibn-ziyad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ubayd-allah-ibn-ziyad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad was an Umayyad governor of Basra and Kufa, the son of Ziyad ibn Abihi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ubayd-zakani"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ubayd-zakani.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ubayd Zakani</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ubayd Zakani was a Persian poet and satirist from Qazvin, active in Shiraz in the 14th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/uberto-pallavicino"><span class="enc-name">Uberto Pallavicino</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Uberto Pallavicino was a Lombard nobleman and leader of the Ghibelline party, a supporter of Emperor Frederick II.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ubiaja"><span class="enc-name">Ubiaja</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ubiaja is a town in Edo State in southern Nigeria, the headquarters of the Esan South-East local government area.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ubii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ubii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ubii</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ubii were a Germanic people who lived on the right bank of the Rhine and became allies of Rome under Julius Caesar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ubs"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ubs.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">UBS</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">UBS is a Swiss bank formed in 1998 by the merger of the Union Bank of Switzerland and the Swiss Bank Corporation, and one of the world's largest wealth managers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ubud"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ubud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ubud</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ubud is a town in Gianyar regency in the uplands of central Bali, Indonesia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ucanal"><span class="enc-name">Ucanal</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ucanal is a Maya archaeological site on the Mopan River in the Petén department of Guatemala.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/uch-sharif"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/uch-sharif.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Uch Sharif</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Uch Sharif is a historic town in the southern Punjab of Pakistan, near the confluence of the Chenab and Sutlej rivers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/uchchala"><span class="enc-name">Uchchala</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Uchchala was a king of Kashmir who founded the second Lohara dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/uckermark"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/uckermark.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Uckermark</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Uckermark is a historical region in the north-east of Brandenburg, Germany, named after the Ucker river, with Prenzlau as its main town.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ucles"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ucles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Uclés</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Uclés is a village in the province of Cuenca, Spain.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udabhandapura"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udabhandapura.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udabhandapura</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Udabhandapura, modern Hund in Pakistan's Khyber Pakhtunkhwa province, was a city on the right bank of the Indus above Attock, at a main crossing of the river.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udai-singh-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udai Singh II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Udai Singh II was Maharana of Mewar until his death in 1572 and the founder of the city of Udaipur in 1559.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-of-dungarpur"><span class="enc-name">Udai Singh of Dungarpur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Rawal Udai Singh was ruler of Vagad, with his capital at Dungarpur in southern Rajasthan, in the early 16th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-of-kaithal"><span class="enc-name">Udai Singh of Kaithal</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Bhai Udai Singh was the last ruler of Kaithal, a Sikh chiefship of the Cis-Sutlej region in present-day Haryana that had accepted British protection in 1809.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udai-singh-of-marwar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udai-singh-of-marwar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udai Singh of Marwar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Udai Singh, known as Mota Raja, was the Rathore ruler of Marwar (Jodhpur) from 1583 to 1595, restored to his kingdom as a vassal of Akbar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udaipur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udaipur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udaipur</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Udaipur is a city in southern Rajasthan, India, founded in 1559 by Maharana Udai Singh II as the new capital of Mewar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udayagiri-and-khandagiri-caves"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udayagiri-and-khandagiri-caves.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udayagiri and Khandagiri Caves</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Udayagiri and Khandagiri Caves are rock-cut chambers in two neighbouring hills near Bhubaneswar in Odisha, carved mostly in the 1st century BCE as dwellings for…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/udayamati"><span class="enc-name">Udayamati</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Udayamati was a queen of the Chaulukya king Bhima I of Gujarat in the 11th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/udayana-of-vatsa"><span class="enc-name">Udayana of Vatsa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Udayana was a king of Vatsa, with its capital at Kaushambi on the Yamuna, in the 6th century BCE, a contemporary of the Buddha.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/udayana-warmadewa"><span class="enc-name">Udayana Warmadewa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Udayana Warmadewa was a king of Bali of the Warmadewa dynasty in the late 10th and early 11th centuries, ruling from the region of Bedulu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/udayasimha-of-jalor"><span class="enc-name">Udayasimha of Jalor</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Udayasimha was the Chahamana king of Jalor in Rajasthan in the first half of the 13th century and the most powerful ruler of his line.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/udaybhan-singh"><span class="enc-name">Udaybhan Singh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Udaybhan Singh was the last ruling Maharaj Rana of Dholpur, a Jat princely state in Rajputana, from 1911.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/udayeshwar-temple"><span class="enc-name">Udayeshwar Temple</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Udayeshwar Temple, also called Neelkantheshwar, is an 11th-century Shiva temple at Udaipur in Vidisha district of Madhya Pradesh, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udayin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udayin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udayin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Udayin, or Udayabhadra, was a king of Magadha of the Haryanka dynasty, the son and successor of Ajatashatru.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udhampur"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udhampur.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udhampur</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Udhampur is a city in the Jammu division of Jammu and Kashmir, India, and the seat of Udhampur district, on the road from Jammu to Srinagar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udine"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udine.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udine</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Udine is a city in north-eastern Italy and the historic capital of Friuli.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udjahorresnet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udjahorresnet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udjahorresnet</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Udjahorresnet was an Egyptian official, physician and priest of Neith at Sais who served the last Saite kings and then the Persian conquerors.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udmurts"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udmurts.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udmurts</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Udmurts are a Finno-Ugric people of the lands between the Vyatka and Kama rivers, most of whom live in the Udmurt Republic of Russia, with its capital at Izhevsk.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/udupi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/udupi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Udupi</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Udupi is a city on the coast of Karnataka, south-western India, known for its Krishna temple and monasteries founded by the 13th-century philosopher Madhvacharya, who…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/uele-river"><span class="enc-name">Uele River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Uele River flows west across the north of the Democratic Republic of the Congo and joins the Mbomu to form the Ubangi, a tributary of the Congo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-harunori"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/uesugi-harunori.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Uesugi Harunori</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Uesugi Harunori, also known as Uesugi Yōzan, was daimyo of the Yonezawa Domain in northern Japan from 1767 to 1785.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kagekatsu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/uesugi-kagekatsu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Uesugi Kagekatsu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Uesugi Kagekatsu was the adopted heir of Uesugi Kenshin, who won the succession war after Kenshin's death in 1578.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Uesugi Kenshin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Uesugi Kenshin was a warlord of Echigo province in the Sengoku period, famous for his generalship and Buddhist devotion.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-norimasa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/uesugi-norimasa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Uesugi Norimasa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Uesugi Norimasa was the Kantō kanrei, the shogun's deputy for eastern Japan, from the Yamanouchi branch of the Uesugi family.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-noritada"><span class="enc-name">Uesugi Noritada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Uesugi Noritada was the Kantō deputy, or kanrei, the chief adviser of the Kamakura kubo in eastern Japan, from the late 1440s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-zenshu-rebellion"><span class="enc-name">Uesugi Zenshū rebellion</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt · 1416–1417</span><span class="enc-snip">The Uesugi Zenshū rebellion was a revolt in the Kanto region of Japan in 1416 and 1417 led by Uesugi Ujinori, known as Zenshū, a former deputy of the Kamakura kubo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ufa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/ufa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ufa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</s